window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][138] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C27 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design the trust and safety approach for a new live-video feature.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add a report button and moderators.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names harms and layers.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Starts from harms and adversaries, designs prevention by design (limits, age and account requirements), real-time detection, human review, appeals and measures prevalence, and plans a staged rollout with a kill switch.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Live video is hard because harm happens in real time and cannot be reviewed first. I'd start with the harms (violence, sexual content, harassment, scams, self-harm) and the adversaries who will adapt. Layers: <em>prevention by design:</em> require account age and verification to broadcast, start with small audiences, limit features for new accounts, delay or sample streams from high-risk accounts; <em>detection:</em> automated checks on audio, video and chat, with a lower threshold for severe harms; <em>response:</em> the ability to interrupt a stream quickly, human review of flagged streams within minutes, and a kill switch per feature; <em>after:</em> appeals and penalties. &quot;Measures: prevalence of violating live views by sampling, time to interrupt, proactive detection rate, appeal overturn rate and legitimate-creator friction. Tradeoffs: a lower threshold stops more harm and interrupts more good streams; I'd set it by harm severity. Rollout: invite-only, then a small share of regions, with review of incidents and red-team tests before expanding.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Harms first; layers designed for real time; prevalence; staged rollout.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c27-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C27-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C27 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You are the PM of an internal platform used by 40 teams. Only 12 have adopted it. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Mandate that teams use it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Finds out why teams haven't adopted.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Segments the non-adopters, measures time to first use and the cost of migration, fixes the biggest barrier, uses early adopters as proof, and considers mandates only with evidence and a transition plan.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Adoption is 30% (12 of 40). I'd interview a sample of non-adopters and look at why: it doesn't fit their use case, migration is too costly, it's less reliable than their own solution, they don't know about it, or they distrust the team's support. Then quantify: time to first successful use, migration effort, and the value they'd get (time saved, incidents avoided). &quot;Fix the largest barrier first: if migration takes three weeks and the benefit is small, build migration tools or support; if it doesn't fit a use case shared by ten teams, add it; if reliability is the issue, publish and meet service levels. Use the 12 adopters as proof: collect results (hours saved) and have them help others. A mandate is a last resort: it produces compliance and resentment, and works only when the benefit is clear and a transition is funded. Measure adoption by workload moved, not by teams signed, with guardrails on reliability and customer satisfaction. I'd also be willing to say no to the teams whose needs are too different, and say so.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Diagnosis of non-adoption; barrier-first; proof; careful use of mandates.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c27-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C27-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C27 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> As a growth PM you raised weekly active users by 12%, but 90-day retention fell. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Keep the growth; retention often drops with more users.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Segments new users and checks quality.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates mix shift from a real change in retention, reads cohorts by acquisition source and tactic, computes the net effect on long-term active users and acquisition payback, checks for dark patterns, and decides by a rule.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;First check whether it's mix or real. If the growth came from a new channel or tactic whose users retain less, overall retention falls even if nothing else changed; compare retention by cohort and source. Then compute the net effect on long-term active users: say a baseline of 100,000 new users with 30% retained at 90 days is 30,000 retained; the growth tactic adds 12,000 new users with 10% retention (1,200 retained) while lowering retention among existing sources by 2 points (2,000 fewer): net negative. If instead the tactic's users retain at 25% and the rest is unchanged, the net is positive. &quot;Then inspect the tactic: did it use incentives, forced sharing or hard-to-cancel flows (dark patterns, C10.10) that inflate sign-ups and harm trust? I'd check complaints and the economics: acquisition cost against the lifetime value of the retained users (C20.7). Decision rule: keep the tactic where the 90-day retained users per dollar beat the baseline channel; cut or redesign where they do not; and change the goal metric to retained users, not weekly active users, so growth is not rewarded for churn.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Mix versus real; net retained users; dark patterns; a rule; a better metric.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c27-09\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C27-09</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C27 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is different about building a product in a regulated domain such as health?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;More rules to follow.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names safety, privacy and evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Designs around outcomes, validates claims, engages regulators and clinicians early, handles sensitive data, plans slow cycles, and applies AI-specific evaluation and human oversight.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Three things change. The goal: outcomes (improved health measures, adherence, fewer adverse events) matter more than engagement; a product that holds attention and doesn't help is a failure. The evidence bar: claims must be supported, and a product that diagnoses or treats may be regulated as a medical device with clinical validation <code>[V: FDA, EU MDR and India's Medical Devices Rules 2017]</code>. The data: health data is among the most sensitive, with strict rules on collection, consent, storage and sharing <code>[V: HHS: HIPAA Privacy Rule and Security Rule apply to covered entities and business associates]</code>. &quot;So I'd involve clinicians, privacy and regulatory experts from the problem stage; define the claim narrowly (information, not diagnosis, unless validated); measure outcomes and safety events; design for clinical workflow, since products that burden clinicians are abandoned; and plan for slow cycles and review before launch. Growth tactics from consumer apps (aggressive notifications, streaks, incentives) can be inappropriate. If AI is involved, I'd add evaluation on real cases with error costs, human oversight for anything consequential, and clear limits shown to users (C24, C25).&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Outcomes; evidence and regulation; sensitive data; clinicians; AI controls.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c28-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C28-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C28 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Pick a product you use every day and critique it in ten minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's great; I'd add more features.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States the purpose, ranks issues and proposes a change with a metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Labels what is verified, inferred and opinion; explains why strengths work; names the cost of the change and what could not be verified.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>(A note-taking app, used as an example; no claim about any real product's internals.)</em> &quot;The app exists to help people capture thoughts quickly and find them later; for the company it serves retention and subscriptions. I'll judge it by how fast I can capture a thought and how reliably I can find it. What works: it opens to a new note, so capture takes one tap (observation), which suits the job because capture is urgent and sorting is not (inference). Ranked issues: first, search misses notes when I misspell (observation, severe for the 'find' job); second, sharing a note requires the other person to sign up (observation); third, I'd like folders to be optional (opinion). My change: forgiving search that handles typos. Metric: the share of searches that end with an opened note within 30 seconds; guardrail: search latency. Cost: indexing and ranking effort. What I can't verify is how many users search at all; I'd check that first.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Purpose; ranked issues with labels; a metric, guardrail and cost.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c28-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C28-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C28 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> In a critique, what is the difference between an observation, an inference and an opinion? Give an example.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Facts versus feelings.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines the three and gives an example.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Applies the layers to a real, sourced example and shows how each is challenged.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;An observation is what I saw or what a source reports. An inference is what I think it implies. An opinion is what I prefer. Example from Spotify's weekly playlist: observation (from the company's reported figures): more than half of listeners returned the next week and saved at least one song; inference: the weekly rhythm builds a habit and saves feed the next list, which also risks narrowing taste; opinion: I'd like a control to steer it. Each is challenged differently. An observation is challenged by asking for the source; an inference by asking for the evidence and the alternative explanation (maybe people return because the list is good, not because of the rhythm); an opinion by asking who else would prefer the opposite.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Clear definitions; a sourced example; how each is tested.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c28-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C28-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C28 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Critique the setup experience of wireless earbuds sold with a phone, and say what you would change.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Pairing should be easier.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the friction removed and the ecosystem effect.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Labels verified claims, ranks issues including ecosystem dependence and repair, proposes a change with metric and cost, and states the hardware constraint.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general and uses public Apple announcements; no internal claim. &quot;Goal: wireless audio that works without fuss with the user's devices; for the company, hardware sales and ecosystem depth; I judge by the speed and reliability of first connection and later switching. Verified from the company's 2016 announcement: opening the case near an iPhone and tapping sets up the earbuds, and they work with all devices signed into the same iCloud account; in-ear sensors pause and play automatically; the launch slipped from late October to orders on 13 December. Inference: pairing was the biggest friction in earbuds and moving it into the chip and the account made setup the differentiator; the account link raises switching cost. Issues, ranked: first (opinion, high), full value depends on owning the company's devices; second (inference), small and easy to lose, with a battery that wears out; third (observation), a hardware component can move a launch. My change: a clear 'replace one earbud' path with transparent pricing. Metrics: replacement rate, support contacts per 1,000 units and satisfaction at twelve months. Cost: inventory and service operations.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Labels; the removed friction; ranked issues; a cost.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c28-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C28-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C28 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Google</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you decide whether to build a travel mode for a specific segment in a new market, such as motorcyclists in a country with many?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;If there are many riders, build it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Sizes the segment and identifies what differs.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Identifies the different routing, speed and cue requirements, estimates reach and accuracy risk, uses a staged launch with metrics and safety guardrails, and cites public evidence carefully.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I use public statements about a similar launch and claim no internal knowledge. &quot;Start with the job: what is different for this segment's trips? Public statements about an India-first mode say that riders use shortcuts closed to cars, move differently in traffic and navigate by landmarks <code>[V: press, 2017]</code>. So three things differ: routing rules, travel-time models and guidance cues. Size: the segment's share of trips in the market, from public vehicle statistics and our own data. Risk: accuracy depends on local data (restrictions, shortcuts) and errors cost trust and safety. A staged plan: launch in a few cities where the data is strongest; measure mode adoption among likely riders, the gap between predicted and actual arrival time, and navigation abandonment; guardrails: safety incident reports and distraction (voice guidance). Reach: if the segment is a large share of trips and the baseline error is high, build; if it's small or the data is thin, use a lighter step (a route preference) first.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> The job; what differs; sizing; a staged plan with safety.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c28-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C28-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C28 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Netflix</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You are rolling out paid sharing for a subscription service. How do you plan it, and what do you tell leadership to expect?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Roll it out and see what happens.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Stages the rollout and sets measures.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Predicts a cancellation reaction, defines success on net additions and revenue, plans by market, communicates clearly to members, and sets guardrails; uses public precedent carefully.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I use public shareholder letters as precedent and claim no internal knowledge. &quot;Set expectations first: a public letter from a streaming company said it expected a 'cancel reaction' in each market when it rolled out paid sharing, based on an earlier experience in Latin America <code>[V: shareholder letter, January 2023]</code>, and later reported that revenue in each region was higher than before launch and sign-ups exceeded cancellations <code>[V: shareholder letter, July 2023]</code>. I'd tell leadership to expect a short-term dip in net additions, a recovery as extra members and new accounts come in, and to judge success on revenue and net additions over several quarters, not on the first month. &quot;Plan: stage by market, starting where sharing is common and the product is strong; communicate clearly (what changes, what it costs, how to add an extra member); offer an easy way to move a profile to its own account; measure cancellations, sign-ups, extra-member adds, complaint rate and retention of the converted accounts; guardrails: member satisfaction and support contacts. Decision rule: pause and adjust a market if cancellations exceed a threshold set in advance or complaints spike.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Expectations; staging; success on revenue and net additions; communication; guardrails.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c28-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C28-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C28 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Meta</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A messaging app encrypts every message so the company cannot read it. How do you fight spam and abuse?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Scan messages for bad content.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses behavior and reports, not content.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Designs signals from behavior and metadata, user reporting, friction, limits and account-level actions, measures prevalence and errors, and states what cannot be detected.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I use a public account of one platform's experience and claim no internal knowledge. &quot;If I cannot read content, I use behavior. A public account from an engineer at an encrypted messenger reported that spam fell by 75% after encryption was introduced because behavioral signals detect spam without content <code>[V: press reporting a 2017 conference talk]</code>. Signals: message volume and speed, the share of recipients who do not know the sender, how many people block or report the sender, the age and pattern of the account and device, and group-joining patterns. Controls: limits on forwarding and bulk messaging, friction for new accounts, easy blocking and reporting (a reported message can be shared with the company by the recipient), account-level penalties and detection of automation. Measures: prevalence of spam by sampling reports, time to action, appeal rate, and false-positive rate on legitimate users, with a guardrail on friction. &quot;What cannot be done: detecting a harm that exists only in content, such as a specific kind of abusive image, without a user report or a content signal the user chooses to share. For those, reporting and metadata carry more weight, and some harms will be detected later or not at all; I'd say so, and make sure policy and law enforcement processes handle what is reported.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Behavior signals; controls; measures; honesty about limits.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c28-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C28-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C28 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A recommendation feed has high return rates, but you suspect it is narrowing what people see. How do you check, and what do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add more variety.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines a diversity metric and tests an intervention.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Measures narrowing over time per user, separates choice from the system's effect, tests exploration with guardrails, reads effects by week, and states the tradeoff with engagement.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd define narrowing as a falling variety of what a user is shown and plays over time, compared with their own earlier behavior and a control group. Measures: the number of distinct artists or topics per user per month, the share of plays from the user's top three, and the share of plays of items new to the user. To separate the system's effect from the user's taste, I'd compare users who got personalized lists with a holdout who got a less personalized mix, and look at the change in variety over weeks (popularity bias and feedback loops, C23.8). &quot;If narrowing shows, the intervention is an exploration share (say 10% of the list from outside the user's pattern) with a steer control; test it with the saves-per-listener metric as primary, discovery as a second primary, and return rate as a guardrail. Sample: about 9,800 per arm to detect a two-point rise in saves from a 50% baseline. Read by week for novelty. The tradeoff: exploratory items are skipped more, so short-term engagement may dip; I'd accept a small dip if discovery and long-term return improve, and set the threshold in advance.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Narrowing defined; a holdout; exploration with guardrails; the tradeoff.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c28-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C28-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C28 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A watch notifies a wearer of a possible irregular heart rhythm. Design what happens after the alert.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Tell them to see a doctor.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Gives clear next steps and handles anxiety.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Designs a guided path using verified study facts, offers confirming options, uses plain language about what the alert means, measures follow-through and distress, and works within regulation and clinical input.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general and uses a published study; no internal claim. &quot;Facts from the published study: 0.52% of participants were notified; the notification had a positive predictive value of 0.84 against a simultaneous monitor patch, but only 34% of notified participants who wore a patch about two weeks later showed the condition, and the authors noted that the condition can come and go, so a later patch can miss it <code>[V: peer-reviewed paper, 2019]</code>. So a person who gets an alert has most likely had a real irregular rhythm, may not show it again soon, and needs to understand both. &quot;Design: plain language ('your watch noticed an irregular rhythm; this is not a diagnosis; it can be worth talking to a clinician'); an immediate choice of a confirming step: record a reading now if the device supports it, book a clinic or telehealth visit, or schedule a monitor; a summary the person can share with a clinician; no alarmist wording and clear urgent-symptom guidance (chest pain, fainting means seek emergency care). Measures: the share who take a confirming step within two weeks, the share who report distress, and the share of alerts followed by a diagnosis (reported with consent); guardrails: false-alert reports and unnecessary visits. Process: clinical review of wording, and regulatory consideration of claims (C27.9).&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Facts used correctly; a guided path; plain language; measures; clinical and regulatory care.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c28-09\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C28-09</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C28 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A bold redesign of search, launched as the company's biggest change in a decade, appears to be hurting users who know exactly what they want. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Roll it back.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Segments by intent and measures the harm.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates the inspiration and fixed-intent jobs, measures the cost to each with a holdout, keeps the default path for the common intent, adds the redesigned path alongside, sets a decision rule, and communicates openly.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;First separate the jobs: guests who want inspiration and guests who know where and when they want to stay. Measure each: search-to-booking conversion, time to a booking, the number of steps, and complaints, by intent segment, with a holdout on the old search where possible. Public commentary on a similar redesign said it forced searching by category and confused location-bound searchers <code>[V: commentary, 2023; opinion]</code>, and the company redesigned its type-of-place filter in 2023 and, according to trade press, ended category search in 2025 <code>[V: Airbnb release; trade press]</code>. I'd treat those as hypotheses to check in our own data. &quot;If the harm to fixed-intent users is real, restore the classic path as the default and keep the new browsing path as an optional surface for people who want inspiration; test that combination against both groups. Decision rule: keep any element that raises inspiration-segment bookings without lowering fixed-intent conversion beyond a stated margin. Communicate openly to hosts and guests what is changing and why. A bold change that gets adjusted after learning is normal; the mistake is leaving a harmed segment unmeasured.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Intent segments; measured harm; a default path; a decision rule; openness.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c29-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C29-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C29 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is a feature factory, and how would you avoid being one?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A team that ships too many features.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> A team judged by output instead of outcomes, with a fix.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Names detection signals (outcome coverage, shipped-and-measured share), explains the root cause, and describes practices with a check.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;A feature factory is a team that is rewarded for shipping features, not for what the features change. It happens because output is visible and outcomes are slow. I'd look for two signals: the share of roadmap items that have an outcome metric and target (if under half, it's a warning), and the share of shipped items that were measured afterward. If 6 of 20 items have metrics and 3 of 14 shipped items were reviewed, the team cannot tell which launches mattered. &quot;To avoid it: every item gets a metric, a baseline, a target and a review date before work begins; I keep an outcomes log; I report outcome coverage and on-time reviews beside velocity; and I stop items whose metric doesn't move after review. The check is a number: in the next quarter I'd want near 100% coverage on new items and over 80% of launches reviewed on time.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> The definition; measurable signals; practices with a check.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c29-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C29-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C29 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is a vanity metric? Give an example and a better alternative.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A metric that looks good but means nothing.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> One that rises without informing decisions, with an example.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Gives a test (would a decision change if it fell?), an example with the better metric and a guardrail, and notes the role of distributions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;A vanity metric is a number that rises reliably and flatters but does not tell you whether the product is working or what to do. My test is: if this number fell, would any decision change? Registered users is the standard example: it only grows. A better metric is weekly active users who return in each of the last four weeks, by cohort, which shows whether people keep coming back. I'd pair it with a guardrail such as complaints or cancellations so that it cannot be raised by tricks. &quot;Averages can also be vanity: a mean session length of 33 seconds can hide that 90% of sessions are 3 seconds. So I'd report the median and a share-above-threshold, such as the share of users with three meaningful sessions a week.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> A decision test; a better metric with a guardrail; distributions.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c29-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C29-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C29 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A senior stakeholder insists you build a feature because a competitor has it. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Build it to stay competitive,&quot; or &quot;refuse.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks about the user and the evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates strategy by analogy from a real gap, checks who is leaving and why, sizes the gap, offers a test, and uses a decision rule while respecting the decider.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd take the concern seriously and ask for the evidence. 'They have it' is strategy by analogy; the questions are whether customers are leaving because we lack it, how many, and what they say. I'd check churn reasons, lost-deal notes and usage of the competitor's feature if public, and ask who would use it and what job it does for them. I'd also ask how the competitor's situation differs from ours, and who has tried it and failed. &quot;Then propose a proportional step: a smoke test or a manual version, with a threshold agreed in advance, and a decision date. If the evidence shows a real gap, build it; if not, document why and keep watching. If the stakeholder decides to build it anyway and it's within their authority, I'd commit, write down my concern and the signal that would make us revisit, and measure the result.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Evidence over analogy; a test with a rule; commitment after the decision.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c29-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C29-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C29 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Users keep asking for a feature. Do you build it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, users know what they want.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks about the underlying need.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates requests from situations, counts who is asking and what share they represent, checks behavior, considers alternative solutions, and tests before building.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd treat the request as a symptom of a job, not as a specification. I'd ask: what are they trying to do when they want it, what do they do today, and how often? I'd count how many are asking and what share of users and revenue they are, because a loud ten may not represent a quiet thousand. I'd check behavior: do those users already work around the problem, and where do they drop? &quot;Then I'd list other ways to meet the need (a setting, a default, a different feature) and test the cheapest one: a fake door to see how many click, or a manual version for five customers. I build the request as asked only if it turns out to be the best way to serve the need and the numbers justify it.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Request versus job; counting and representation; alternatives; a cheap test.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c29-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C29-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C29 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your launch hit its date, but the metric did not move. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Wait and see,&quot; or &quot;ship another feature.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Checks adoption and measurement.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Diagnoses along the funnel (awareness, activation, value), checks the metric and holdout design, reads effects by week and segment, decides to iterate, stop or extend, and records the learning.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;First check that the measurement is sound: is the metric computed correctly, is there a holdout, and is the sample large enough? Then diagnose along the path: did users find the feature (awareness), try it (activation), and get value (repeat use)? If adoption is 4% against a 15% goal, the problem is awareness or activation, with different fixes: placement and messaging, or a simpler first use. If adoption is fine and the metric did not move, the feature may not affect the outcome, and the assumption linking them was wrong. &quot;Read by week and segment: a novelty bump that faded, or an effect in one segment that is diluted in the average. Decide by the criteria set before launch: iterate if there is a fixable path, stop if the assumption failed (and free the capacity), extend the test if it is underpowered. Then record the learning in the outcomes log.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Measurement check; funnel diagnosis; segments and weeks; a decision rule; a record.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c29-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C29-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C29 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How do you spot your own recurring mistakes?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I reflect after projects.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Reviews artifacts and asks for feedback.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Runs a scan on artifacts with detection signals, counts patterns, finds the root-cause cluster, chooses one practice with a retest, and uses outside examples.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I scan evidence, not memory. I take my last three specs, my roadmap with its outcome measures, my mock or review scorecards and my stories, and check each against a list of detection signals: for example, does each roadmap item have a metric, did I state the guardrail, did the story show a decision. I count how often each pattern appears, group them by root cause (activity mistaken for progress, optimizing the measure, avoiding discomfort, pattern-matching, not closing the loop), and look for the dominant cluster. &quot;Then one practice, not five: say, 'I name the guardrail in the same sentence as the metric.' I apply it to the next artifacts and recheck in three or four weeks. I also ask a teammate for one example of each pattern they've seen me do, since I can't see all of them. Last time my scan showed that in 6 of 8 mocks I gave no guardrail; after the practice and ten drills, it was 1 in 4.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Evidence; counts; a cluster; one practice and a retest; outside views.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c29-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C29-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C29 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A product has 40 million registered users, but most sessions are very short. Is it healthy?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;40 million users is great.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Questions the registered number and asks about engagement.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes how mean and median differ, defines meaningful use, asks how sessions arise (intent or incidental), reads cohorts and retention, and sets decision criteria.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Not necessarily. Registered users counts everyone who ever signed up and says nothing about use. If 90% of sessions last about 3 seconds and 10% last about 300, the mean is 32.7 seconds, which looks fine, but the median is 3 seconds and 92% of the time comes from 10% of sessions. So I'd measure the median and the share of users with repeated meaningful sessions, say three sessions of at least 30 seconds a week, and retention by cohort. &quot;I'd ask how short sessions arise: from users who intend to be there, or from links, notifications or integrations in other products that land them there? Incidental sessions are not interest. I'd also check who the long sessions are: a loyal minority is a possible niche; no one is a problem. Decision criteria: if fewer than, say, a tenth of registered users meet the engagement bar and cohort retention falls to near zero, the issue is not growth but a missing reason to return, and I'd narrow to the segment with real use or stop.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Distribution; meaningful use; origin of sessions; cohorts; a decision rule.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c29-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C29-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C29 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You discover your team has been optimizing a metric that does not matter for the last six months. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Switch metrics and move on.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Tells the team and leadership and resets the goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Verifies the finding, owns your part, quantifies what was lost, tells stakeholders early with a plan, reconstructs which work still matters, sets the right metric with a guardrail and a review date, and changes the practice that allowed it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;First verify: what exactly doesn't matter, and what is the evidence (no relation to retention or revenue in cohorts, say). Then tell my manager and the team promptly, in person and then in writing: here is what we found, what it cost (say, six months of two-thirds of our effort), what I think we should do and when I'll update. I'd own my part, since I helped set the goal, without self-flagellation. &quot;Then reset: choose the metric that tracks value (a retained-user measure, with a guardrail), set a baseline and a target, and review the past six months of work against it to see what still helps: some of it likely does. Decide what to stop, keep and reshape, with numbers. And I'd change the practice: every goal metric gets a documented link to an outcome, checked quarterly against cohorts, so this can't recur silently.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Verification; early disclosure with a plan; ownership; salvage; a changed practice.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c30-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C30-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C30 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you take a new product idea from discovery to launch?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Research, design, build, launch.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Stages with evidence and a test before the build.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Names the riskiest assumption, a cheap test with thresholds set first, a spec with non-goals, a metric tree with guardrails, a staged rollout and a review after launch.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd stage it so that spend follows evidence. Discovery: understand the user and the problem with interviews, observation and data, and list the assumptions; the riskiest is usually that people will pay or change behavior. Alpha: test that assumption with the cheapest experiment that can fail, such as a smoke test or a manual version, with the thresholds written first (proceed, stop, and what a middle result triggers). Spec: a short document with the problem statement, goals, non-goals and acceptance criteria. Metrics: a tree with a primary measure, the drivers and a guardrail. Beta: a staged rollout, private then wider, with a rollback trigger. Live: a review at a set date against the success criteria, and a retrospective that records the pattern and what I'd change. &quot;At each gate I'd decide to proceed, persist, pivot or stop by the rules written before the evidence arrived.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Evidence-gated stages; thresholds first; non-goals; a review.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c30-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C30-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C30 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is the difference between discovery, alpha, beta and live?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Early, middle and late stages.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> The purpose of each.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> States the evidence needed to exit each phase, the decision at each gate and that the live phase keeps reviewing; notes that public-sector digital teams use this model.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Discovery is for understanding users, constraints and the problem; its exit is evidence of the problem, the riskiest assumptions and a plan for testing them. Alpha is for testing different solutions to those assumptions, usually with prototypes; its exit is a tested solution with results against thresholds. Beta builds the best option and tests it with real users, first a limited private group and then everyone; its exit is a working end-to-end service with metrics, guardrails and operational readiness. Live runs and keeps improving the service, with reviews of outcomes and a plan for the next cycle. Public-sector digital teams, such as the UK government's, publish this four-phase model with assessments at gates, and it maps well onto product work.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Purpose and exit evidence for each; gates; continued review.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c30-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C30-03</h3>\n";
